  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 106 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nOn Sunday, the remnants of \"Isaias\" is expected to be tracking up \nthe Mississippi Delta Region.  The circulation from this post-\ntropical system will be building a ridge upstream/westward across \nthe Southern Plains which will result in hotter daytime temperatures \nfor Sunday and Monday. Our area could see highs in the lower to mid \n90s for Sunday & Monday and warmer nights which will be near record-\nbreaking for this time of the year.  A system moving through the \nmain jet stream across the Northeastern U.S. may push the first \nof two cold fronts across our area for next week, with the latter\nmuch stronger at the end of the week. Deterministic models are \nnot consistent with the handling of the first front with the \nECMWF & NBM pushing it into our area Tuesday before stalling out \nacross southwest Oklahoma while the GFS stalls it out up in \nKansas. Based on the first solution, much of our area could see a \nslight cool-down Tuesday from hot to still unseasonably warm with\nhighs in the lower to mid 80s although our southwest CWA could stay\nhot. There is also a 15-20% chance for scattered rain on Tuesday \nwith this first potential frontal passage. Rain & Storm chances \nincrease becoming more widespread on Wednesday as a large Pacific-\nbased system starts digging across the U.S. Southwest toward the\nSouthern Plains.",
